# Working artifacts, never source.
#
# staging/ is where the PC-side tools/ scripts expect to find everything they operate on:
# downloaded release artifacts, extracted kernel images, DTB dumps of the stock firmware, and
# the md5-verified rollback set. It is 431 MiB on this machine and the single largest file in
# it is a 33.5 MB decompressed stock kernel. None of it belongs in a repository - and note
# that a clone cannot run most of these scripts anyway, because the tools assume a populated
# staging/ next to them. Building that directory is the reader's first job, not the clone's.
staging/

# Vendor kernel modules recovered from the stock SK-D840N firmware: 18 binaries, 7.2 MiB.
# These are ZTE/Skyworth proprietary objects, not something this project wrote and not
# something the licence permits redistributing. They are kept on the machine that pulled
# them out of the flash; the reverse-engineering notes that describe them (INVENTORY.md,
# abi-scan.txt and friends) are tracked, which is the part that can be shared.
pon/vendor-modules/

# Python bytecode from running the tools/ scripts.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# tests/fixtures/stale-set/ holds a *fetched* release artifact, not something this repository
# wrote: round36-make-fixture.sh pulls four files from the skd840n_flash_21_38243906799
# release, the largest being a 4.0 MiB jffs2 rootfs. Only the 85-byte sha256 pin is tracked,
# which is the part that says what the fixture has to be - without it the other three files
# cannot be checked at all, and with it a reader can rebuild the set byte for byte.
#
# Written as a rule rather than as a habit on purpose: `git add tests/` on a machine that has
# run the fetch script would otherwise stage 4 MiB of release artifact in one keystroke, and
# the person doing it would not notice until the push.
tests/fixtures/stale-set/*
!tests/fixtures/stale-set/sha256sums-release
